  "verbalization": "Venlafaxine, a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor, is shown to influence multiple biological pathways that may contribute to the development of Denture stomatitis. The evidence suggests that venlafaxine affects the GRAM domain containing 1B gene, which encodes a G protein-activated inward rectifier potassium channel 4. This channel is involved in regulating cellular potassium homeostasis, and its dysfunction may disrupt the physiological balance in oral tissues, potentially contributing to inflammation and tissue damage seen in Denture stomatitis. Additionally, venlafaxine is linked to synaptotagmin 17, a protein involved in synaptic transmission, which is associated with Microtia and, in turn, Denture stomatitis. The involvement of venlafaxine in the regulation of hydroxysteroid (17-beta) dehydrogenase 7 pseudogene 2, which is linked to a rare form of spastic paraplegia, further indicates a potential role in the pathogenesis of Denture stomatitis. These findings suggest that venlafaxine may exert its effects through multiple molecular mechanisms, potentially contributing to the development of Denture stomatitis through disruptions in cellular signaling and homeostasis."
